Yes. That first line is almost the bodily version of the whole architecture:

It’s hard to feel lucky when it’s cold.

The external facts may be identical, but the body changes the prior. Cold, fatigue, hunger, pain, stress—each can alter the lens through which the same situation is valued. “Lucky” is therefore not only a property of circumstances; it is partly a state-dependent interpretation of circumstances.

And the second part describes something stronger than ordinary optimization.

You didn’t just want a system that tries to produce an effect:

input → model → action → desired outcome

You wanted one whose actions are also probes:

input → hypothesis → action/probe → effect + information → delta → model update

That is very close to dual control in control theory: choose actions both to control the system and to learn more about the system you are controlling.

So the objective becomes two-dimensional:

maximize useful effect
and
maximize useful information gained from the resulting delta

Not every successful action is equally valuable. If two actions achieve the same result, but one reveals much more about the hidden system, the second can be better.

And conversely, even a failed action can be valuable if its failure sharply eliminates hypotheses.

That gives Pantheon something like an epistemic exhaust recovery system: don’t waste the information produced by acting.

The shorthand/body-language/interface piece fits because you’re deliberately increasing the number of channels from which the model can reconstruct intent:

literal words

* timing
* metaphor
* recurring handles
* sequence
* contrast
* omission
* relational context
    → latent intended operator

“Cheating” in the useful sense is really cross-channel inference: if the literal channel is insufficient, use every lawful contextual signal available to reconstruct the intended transformation rather than pretending the text exists in isolation.

But the safeguard is exactly what you said:

No internal model gets promoted to reality merely because it is elegant.

It has to survive contact.

So the runtime becomes:

compress → infer → act → observe → measure ontic delta → update

And importantly:

small delta → calibration
persistent delta → model revision
unexpected useful delta → Recursive Serendipity
large structural delta → Semantic Phase Transition

That gives you a system designed not merely to be right, but to become less wrong efficiently while doing useful work.

There’s even a subtle improvement beyond “optimize for effect + information”: optimize for identifiability. Choose interventions whose possible outcomes discriminate strongly between competing explanations.

If hypotheses A and B both predict the same result, the action teaches you little.

If A predicts X and B predicts Y, reality can answer the question for you.

So the strongest formulation might be:

Act so that success creates value, failure creates information, and either outcome improves the next act.

That is much closer to what you’ve actually been building than simple optimization.